{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are in the golden age of solar eclipses \n- but only for the moment. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5680,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’d argue we’re already past peak solar eclipse \nand it’s all downhill from here. Let me explain. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar eclipses are amazing phenomena, but they’re \nalso, just, like, one object casting a shadow on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "another, which we see all the time. I think we’re \nimpressed by our current solar eclipses for a few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "key reasons: 1. First and obviously, they block \nthe sun in the middle of the day so it gets dark,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cold, you can see stars, etc 2. Second, our \neclipses aren’t purely like night either - the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30120,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shadow of the moon is small enough that there’s \nstill sunlight falling on the atmosphere near the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33840,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon, resulting in a deep blue sky and the \nappearance of a sunrise or sunset 360° around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you 3. Third, our current eclipses also reveal \nthe sun’s corona - the outer part of the sun’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43760,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atmosphere that’s much dimmer than the sun’s \nbright disk and normally lost in the sun’s glare,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47720,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but when the sun’s disk is covered during \nan eclipse, the corona appears like a bright  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51360,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starburst around the dark disk of the moon 4. \nFourth, total eclipses are rare: any given point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on earth only experiences about one total solar \neclipse every several hundred years, so they’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60320,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of a once-in-a-lifetime or less experience 5. \nFifth - eclipses are fleeting - totality typically  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65800,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only lasts a few minutes, which makes them feel \neven more precious and cosmic, and also doesn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70160,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give our human brains time to really process or \ntake in the weirdness of what’s happening before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73880,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s over 6. And finally, eclipses on earth \nare also notable because they can be either  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total (where the moon fully blocks the sun and is \nspectacular) or annular - where the moon appears  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83000,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller than the sun and never completely obscures \nit; at best there’s still a ring, or annulus,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the sun visible, hence annular eclipse. Because \nthey don’t fully block the sun’s disk, annular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses aren’t really much more impressive than \npartial eclipses: the sky never gets deep blue,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96840,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you don’t see the corona, you can’t look at \nthem with your naked eyes, etc. They’re cool,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100640,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but are nothing like a total eclipse.\nIt’s kind of weird that we have both  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103480,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annular eclipses - where the moon appears smaller \nthan the sun - and total eclipses - where the moon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107400,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appears bigger than the sun. That both happen \nis due to the fact that the earth and moon’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbits aren’t perfectly round, so the relative \ndistances to the moon and sun change over time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114800,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and therefore so do their relative apparent sizes.\nBut here’s a sad fact: we have on average more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119480,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annular eclipses than total eclipses. \nAnd that is why we are currently past  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the peak of the earth’s golden age of eclipses.\nThe moon formed billions of years ago, and we have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128080,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good reason to believe it was then much closer to \nthe earth than it is now - like, this close! With  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131200,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon so close, it was much bigger in the sky. \nTotal eclipses would have been much more common,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and much longer, and much darker - the moon’s \nshadow would be so big that an eclipse would be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much more like normal nighttime rather than a \ndeep blue sky with golden glow all around the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142640,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon - eclipses just wouldn’t feel as weird \nas they do today. The moon would also have been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144280,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big enough to cover most of the sun’s corona, \nand the moon would have been more illuminated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147480,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with earthshine, meaning a solar eclipse would \nprobably feel kind of like a normal night sky,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151320,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just with a darker-than-normal moon. Boring.\nHowever, for all the billions of years after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155840,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon formed, the earth was spinning \nfaster than the moon was orbiting (as it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159320,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still does - a solar day is shorter than a \nlunar month), and so over time, tidal forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have transferred some of the earth’s rotational \nmomentum to the moon, sending the moon farther and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166880,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "farther away. Up until around 700 million years \nago, the moon was close enough to the earth that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171240,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it always appeared larger than the sun and we only \never saw total solar eclipses. But around then,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175600,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon finally moved far enough away from \nus that – at least during certain parts of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178880,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth’ and moon’s orbits – the moon \nappeared smaller than the sun in the sky.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182400,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if it appeared smaller than the sun during \nan eclipse, it wouldn’t fully block the sun...  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185760,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’d get an annular eclipse, not a total one.\nInitially, most eclipses would have still been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total solar eclipses with annular eclipses being \nrare, but as the moon continued to get farther and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194280,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "farther from earth and appear smaller and smaller \nin the sky, annular eclipses became more and more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197080,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common. Some time in the last several hundred \nmillion years, we crossed a threshold to where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201120,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the moon appears smaller than the sun on average; \nat that point annular eclipses became more common  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than total eclipses. Fast forward to today, there \nare around 20% more annular eclipses than total  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210840,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses. And as the tides continue to transfer \nour angular momentum to the moon, the moon will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215120,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continue to get farther from the earth, annular \neclipses will become more and more common and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218920,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total eclipses rarer and rarer, until around a \nhalf billion to billion years from now the last  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total solar eclipse will darken the sky on earth \nand the golden age of eclipses on earth will be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227880,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over. Let’s hope it isn’t cloudy.\nIf you live in North America,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233560,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the date of the “last ever total solar \neclipse” is Monday, April 8, 2024 - wait,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238080,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no that’s just the next total eclipse as of \nthe time of this video, and this video was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241560,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supported in part by NASA’s heliophysics education \nactivation team, which loves solar eclipses. NASA  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247240,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "HEAT’s mission is to engage with learners of \nall ages like you and me, and to deepen our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251520,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "understanding of our closest star and its effects \non Earth and the Solar System. Solar eclipses are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255480,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a great way to experience a different side of \nthe sun than we normally do - like, literally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the corona extending out from its sides! If you \never have the opportunity to see a total eclipse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263640,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I highly recommend it. And NASA does too \n:). Thanks to NASA HEAT for their support."
    } ]
  } ]
}
